## Development

{% if template == "vals-ai" %}Run `make install`, then `make dev`. The development target saves a snapshot: a fixed copy of your task files. It writes the snapshot's ID to `.local/dataset-ref.txt` and starts the service at `http://127.0.0.1:8001`. Local development uses local storage without authentication. Do not expose this process through an unrestricted public URL.

Check that the server grades the saved example:

```bash
DATASET_REF="$(<.local/dataset-ref.txt)"
curl --fail http://127.0.0.1:8001/evaluate-response/ \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -H "X-Vals-Dataset-Ref: $DATASET_REF" \
  -d '{"task_id":"example-task-2","response":"4"}'
```

The result should contain `"score": 1.0`. Keep the same `X-Vals-Dataset-Ref` header for every request in a run and any grading retries. Editing task files does not change that saved version. After editing, run `make snapshot` and load the new ID for a new run. Run `make check` to test loading, private answer separation, and grading against saved versions. These tests make no model calls.

For Valkyrie, expose the local process through a protected tunnel, then use `valkyrie config service set {{ benchmark_name }} <protected-service-url>`. Follow the onboarding guide to list tasks and run an agent, passing the saved dataset header and your tunnel's authentication header. This example supports text-response grading; implement `evaluate_instance` before running sandbox agents.

For hosted use, configure authenticated shared storage using the [dataset service integration guide](https://github.com/vals-ai/vals-datasets/blob/main/docs/service-integration.md). Supply access settings, the route mapping, and a writable cache. The shared library handles loading and authorization. The Docker build excludes local snapshots and task files. Build it with `docker build --secret id=netrc,src="$HOME/.netrc" -t {{ benchmark_name }} .`, using a netrc file with GitHub credentials that can read the dataset library. BuildKit mounts this credential only during dependency installation; it is not copied into the image.
{% else %}```bash
make install   # install dependencies
make dev       # run local server
make test      # run tests
make help      # list all commands
```
{% endif %}
## Releasing

Versions come from git tags (hatch-vcs). To enable the release flow in this repo:

- Add a **`GH_PAT`** repository secret — a personal access token with permission to push tags (`contents: write`). `auto-tag-release` uses it to push the version tag on merge to `main`; the workflow fails without it.
- Every PR title must include **`#patch`**, **`#minor`**, or **`#major`** — `check-pr-title` enforces this and the tag is bumped accordingly on merge.

Until the first tag exists, the package builds at version `0.0.0`.
